@@ -176,3 +176,32 @@ The existing `viewer/factory.html` now supports a **separately launched trusted
 The C++17 `galaxy_factory_local_host` executable now serves the existing Factory Designer and its trusted colony construction API **from one native process**. Players no longer need to launch the Node.js development server to make real, journaled factory construction steps. It opens only an existing SQLite world, binds a session-restricted **127.0.0.1** listener, and packs the viewer assets alongside its binary. The default browser opens automatically; Windows can use an OS save picker when no `--db` is supplied. Linux developer example: `./build/galaxy_factory_local_host --db /path/to/EXISTING.sqlite`. The original `galaxy_factory_host_bridge` and Node test server remain for compatibility.
 
 The new Linux code passes **60/60 non-live and 26/26 live/relay CTests**, **93/93 JavaScript tests**, **3/3 design tests**, and native HTTP/restart/cancellation checks; these are two disjoint native runs, not a single uninterrupted one. Offline Chromium UI passed, but direct native-browser localhost navigation is blocked by the container policy, and Windows compilation/interactive acceptance is not yet complete. **This is not yet integrated into the main space-game GUI, and walls/doors/floors and NPC construction crews remain future work.** See [the engineering report](docs/engineering/ECON02B2d4c3_Native_Factory_Runtime_2026-10-31.md).
+
+### ECON-02B2d4c4 — Factory construction inside the live colony session
+
+`./scripts/run_colony_linux.sh` launches the colony-first development GUI. Once a
+colony outpost has been constructed and material modules exist, select **Open
+Factory Designer** from the colony panel. The factory page selects an outpost
+belonging to the currently running local player session; imports and explicit
+construction steps execute on **that same C++ PersistentWorld** via the
+allowlisted Python localhost relay. Return to the colony page without launching
+a competing database writer. No Node.js is needed for this gameplay path.
+The relay is still a development dependency for the colony GUI, not a final
+one-click installer or authenticated multiplayer server.
+
+The live colony host, standalone native factory host, and legacy factory bridge
+now cooperate through a per-world OS writer lease (`.sgwriter.lock`). Only one of
+these participating hosts can mutate a given SQLite world at a time; the host
+releases its lease on process exit or relay shutdown. A factory `STEP` advances
+one authoritative committed tick and publishes its corresponding SGVIEW frame
+before the regular colony tick. The editor continues to treat walls, doors, and
+floors as **design-only overlays**; physical fixture construction is a later
+milestone. For an HTTP/SQLite integrated acceptance test run:
+
+```sh
+ctest --test-dir build -R galaxy_econ02b2d4c4_integrated_colony_factory --output-on-failure
+```
+
+An additional `scripts/factory_integrated_browser_smoke.py` performs actual
+Chromium clicks where localhost browsing is permitted. Existing saves are not
+created or modified by either acceptance harness (they use temporary fixtures).
