@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# Single Galaxy — ECON-02B2d4c4 Integrated Colony Factory
+
+**Date:** October 31, 2026 (continuation of EvEmu Convo 06 / 07)
+**Project:** Single Galaxy (independent original game; not EVEmu server code)
+**Source baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d4c3_Native_Factory_Source_Tests_2026-10-31.zip`
+**Scope:** Connect player-visible factory planning with the *same running* colony simulation and SQLite save, without competing authors, phantom materials, or local-browser privilege escalation.
+
+## Implemented
+
+1. **One authoritative world.** `galaxy_colony_live_host` owns the only `PersistentWorld` and associated `HostBlueprintRegistry` during colony gameplay. The existing Python localhost relay passes allowlisted, session-authenticated requests to that same C++ host using framed stdin commands and framed stderr responses. The Python relay **never opens SQLite**. No second native factory game instance is launched from the colony UI.
+2. **One OS-backed writer lease.** `src/world_owner_lock.hpp` applies an exclusive lock to a per-world `.sgwriter.lock` file before world open. The colony host, standalone native factory host, and legacy factory bridge cooperate in this lease. Linux uses `flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)`; Windows uses `LockFileEx(LOCKFILE_FAIL_IMMEDIATELY)` with an overlapped byte-range lock. The lock is released on process exit and is independent of SQLite's own transaction locks. **This protects only entry points that actually participate in the lease**; arbitrary external SQLite writers must still be excluded by deployment policy.
+3. **Bounded and authorized factory IPC.** `src/factory_colony_protocol.hpp` implements `SITES`, `HELLO`, `LIST`, `REGISTER`, `STATUS`, `STEP`, `CANCEL`. The *native host* derives the player, founding ship and ownership from its own colony session. A browser-selected outpost is only an input selector, checked against outposts owned by the active colony ship. Plan IDs are checked against the selected site before use. Layouts are canonical-decoded and validated by the original native registry, not the relay or browser. IDs cannot mint arbitrary host actions.
+4. **No tick/frame desynchronization.** The registry's `STEP` invokes one authoritative `PersistentWorld.step()`. The colony host immediately emits that committed tick's `SGVIEW` frame before continuing with the ordinary simulation step. This fixes a real initial failure (`observation must follow one committed tick`) found during live testing. Existing world events and real material consumption remain authoritative.
+5. **Main gameplay navigation.** `viewer/interactive.html` adds **Open Factory Designer** within colony mode. The factory editor page uses `/factory/api/*` on the same relay, offers selection of genuine outpost IDs, and provides **Return to live colony**. When no outpost exists, explicit construction controls are disabled and the player is instructed to first build an outpost. The standalone Node-free native factory server and offline editor keep their existing paths.
+6. **Clean process lifetime.** The Python relay now unwinds through child cleanup on SIGTERM; the C++ colony host's relay-owned mode exits when its stdin owner disappears, including abrupt process termination, releasing the SQLite world writer lease. This repairs a second real failure found during testing, where an orphaned colony child retained the writer lease.
+7. **Cross-platform compile consideration.** The new writer-lock header includes Winsock2 before Windows.h to avoid the old `winsock.h`/`winsock2.h` conflict in the existing native HTTP host. Windows build and interactive acceptance remain **unverified**, however.
+
+## Verification results (actual tests)
+
+| Gate | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Complete Linux CMake build of all targets | PASS |
+| CTest indices 1–25 | **25/25 PASS** |
+| All B70 colony and all ECON-02B2d4 factory tests (26–54, 82–87) | **35/35 PASS** |
+| CTest indices 55–81 | **27/27 PASS** |
+| Native CTest aggregate, three disjoint selections | **87/87 PASS** (not a single uninterrupted invocation) |
+| JavaScript `node --test` tests excluding two argument-requiring standalone integration drivers | **93/93 PASS** |
+| Both argument-requiring Node integration drivers, via registered CTest | PASS |
+| Offline real Chromium factory editor design / export / import / restore | PASS |
+| Real HTTP to same living C++ world: site listing, hello, plans, plan register, step, cancel, restart | PASS |
+| Forged Origin, incorrect session token, forged owner, foreign site, duplicate IDs, malformed layout | DENIED |
+| Large 300 KB malformed layout spanning the child pipe | DENIED; colony stays running |
+| Concurrent standalone factory host while colony active | DENIED by OS writer lease |
+| Concurrent colony host while standalone factory active | DENIED by OS writer lease |
+| Relay SIGTERM and colony restart | PASS; no stale process-held lease |
+| Chromium **real loopback GUI integration** | **BLOCKED by environment**: `net::ERR_BLOCKED_BY_ADMINISTRATOR` at `http://127.0.0.1:.../viewer/interactive.html`; not a verified GUI success |
+| Windows native compilation and click-through | NOT RUN |
+
+The **live browser acceptance script** is `scripts/factory_integrated_browser_smoke.py`. It builds a disposable fixture, navigates the actual colony page, enters the factory, authorizes a blueprint, constructs one step, cancels and returns to the colony. Run on a system with local Chromium navigation enabled; the container's browser sandbox disallows this step. The HTTP/SQLite path is separately verified without a browser.
+
+## Run locally (development GUI)
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
+cmake --build build -j 4
+./scripts/run_colony_linux.sh build/galaxy_colony_live_host /path/to/colony.sqlite
+# Open http://127.0.0.1:8766/viewer/interactive.html
+# In colony UI: build an outpost, then Open Factory Designer
+ctest --test-dir build -R galaxy_econ02b2d4c4_integrated_colony_factory --output-on-failure
+```
+
+The current colony GUI still uses the existing Python development relay. It is not yet a single-click native desktop shell or an Internet/multiplayer server. Node is used as a developer test harness only; no Node.js process is involved in the integrated gameplay runtime.
+
+## Safety and design boundaries
+
+- Browser and editor do not supply the player, ship ID, owner, world filename, SQL, trusted command ID, or materials. A site selector cannot grant site ownership.
+- Existing outpost and finite manufactured modules are required before equipment construction; partial work and cancellation are durable. Cancellation never refunds already-installed machines.
+- The OS writer lease is cooperative among the integrated native entry points, not a global solution against arbitrary third-party database clients or malicious processes with filesystem access.
+- The persistent colony simulation keeps operating as the user switches pages. Factory construction does not cause a new world, new founding ship or duplicate NPCs.
+- Wall, door and floor primitives are still **blueprint/design overlays**. No claim of installed collidable structures, sealed compartments or material-costed structural construction is made in this milestone.
+- NPC construction labor, wall/floor collision and logistics accounting, power and crew scheduling remain open design/development gates. Unbounded factory layout design, five physical machinery tiers and remote deterministic quantitative production remain unchanged.
+
+## Next milestone
+
+**ECON-02B2d5 — physical construction fixtures**: introduce persistent wall, door and floor world objects through authoritative native recipes, placement collision checks, material reservations, one-step construction journal, replay-safe cancellation, load/restart and off-screen visibility tests. Only afterward delegate legitimate construction to NPC crews with finite staffing, resources, time and labor proficiency.
+
+**Repository discipline:** GitHub is a repository and milestone archive only, not a developer run environment. All changes and tests were performed locally. The Windows-specific compilation and GUI acceptance gates remain open until a real Windows run produces evidence.
